"Molecular Microbiology | Microbiology Journal | Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2958.2009.06658.x)\nciteturn24view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1365-2958.2009.06658.x\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 501\nL0:   * [Button: Skip to Article Content]\nL1:   * [Button: Skip to Article Information]\nL2: \nL3: [Input: Search]Search within[Select]\nL4: \nL5:   * Search term[Input: Search][Input]\nL6: \nL7: cite0†Advanced Search cite1†Citation Search L8: \nL9:   * Search term[Input: Search]\nL10: \nL11: cite2†Advanced Search cite3†Citation Search L12: \nL13: Login / Register\nL14: \nL15: cite4†Volume 72, Issue 2 pp. 479-490\nL16: \nL17: Free Access\nL18: # Functional asymmetry of the F_{0} motor in bacterial ATP synthases\nL19: \nL20: cite5†Alexander Wiedenmann ,\nL21: \nL22: Alexander Wiedenmann\nL23: \nL24: Present address: Institute of Organic Chemistry, University of Zurich, Winterthurerstrasse 190, CH-8057 Zurich, Switzerland.\nL25: \nL26: cite5†Search for more papers by this author L27: \nL28: cite6†Peter Dimroth ,\nL29: \nL30: Peter Dimroth\nL31: \nL32: Institut für Mikrobiologie, ETH Zürich, Wolfgang-Pauli-Str. 10, CH-8093 Zürich, Switzerland.\nL33: \nL34: cite6†Search for more papers by this author L35: \nL36: cite7†Christoph Von Ballmoos ,\nL37: Corresponding Author\nL38: \nL39: Christoph Von Ballmoos\nL40: \nL41: Department of Biochemistry and Biophysics, Arrhenius Laboratories for Natural Sciences, Stockholm University, SE-106 91 Stockholm, Sweden.\nL42: \nL43: *E-mail cite8†[email protected] ; Tel. (+46) 8 162715; Fax (+46) 8 153679.cite7†Search for more papers by this author L44: \nL45: cite5†Alexander Wiedenmann ,\nL46: \nL47: Alexander Wiedenmann\nL48: \nL49: Present address: Institute of Organic Chemistry, University of Zurich, Winterthurerstrasse 190, CH-8057 Zurich, Switzerland.\nL50: cite5†Search for more papers by this author L51: \nL52: cite6†Peter Dimroth ,\nL53: \nL54: Peter Dimroth\nL55: \nL56: Institut für Mikrobiologie, ETH Zürich, Wolfgang-Pauli-Str. 10, CH-8093 Zürich, Switzerland.\nL57: \nL58: cite6†Search for more papers by this author L59: \nL60: cite7†Christoph Von Ballmoos ,\nL61: \nL62: Corresponding Author\nL63: \nL64: Christoph Von Ballmoos\nL65: \nL66: Department of Biochemistry and Biophysics, Arrhenius Laboratories for Natural Sciences, Stockholm University, SE-106 91 Stockholm, Sweden.\nL67: *E-mail cite9†[email protected] ; Tel. (+46) 8 162715; Fax (+46) 8 153679.cite7†Search for more papers by this author L68: \nL69: First published: 07 April 2009\nL70: \nL71: cite10†https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2958.2009.06658.xDigital Object Identifier (DOI)†doi.org L72: \nL73: view metrics\nL74: \nL75: Sections\nL76: \nL77: CITE\nL78: \nL79: cite11†Tools L80: \nL81: Share full-text access\nL82: \nL83: Please review our cite12†Terms and Conditions of Use and check box below to share full-text version of article.\nL84: \nL85: [Input]I have read and accept the Wiley Online Library Terms and Conditions of Use\nL86: \nL87: * * *\nL88: Shareable Link\nL89: \nL90: Use the link below to share a full-text version of this article with your friends and colleagues. cite13†Learn more. L91: \nL92: [Button: Copy URL]\nL93: \nL94: Share a link\nL95: \nL96: Share on\nL97: ## Summary\nL98: F_{1}F_{0} ATP synthases use the electrochemical potential of H^{+} or Na^{+} across biological membranes to synthesize ATP by a rotary mechanism. In bacteria, the enzymes can act in reverse as ATP-driven ion pumps creating the indispensable membrane potential.\nL99: Here, we demonstrate that the F_{0} parts of a Na^{+}- and H^{+}-dependent enzyme display major asymmetries with respect to their mode of operation, reflected by the requirement of ∼100 times higher Na^{+} or H^{+} concentrations for the synthesis compared with the hydrolysis of ATP. A similar asymmetry is observed during ion transport through isolated F_{0} parts, indicating different affinities for the binding sites in the a/c interface.\nL100: Together with further data, we propose a model that provides a rationale for a differential usage of membrane potential and ion gradient during ATP synthesis as observed experimentally. The functional asymmetry might also reflect an important property of the ATP synthesis mechanism in vivo. In Escherichia coli, we observed respiratory chain-driven ATP production at pH 7–8, while P-site pH values < 6.5 were required for ATP synthesis in vitro.\nL101: This discrepancy is discussed with respect to the hypothesis that during respiration lateral proton diffusion could lead to significant acidification at the membrane surface.\nL102: ## Introduction\nL103: In almost all living cells, the majority of ATP is synthesized by the ATP synthase, a multisubunit molecular machine. It is composed of two independent motors: the water soluble F_{1} and the membrane embedded F_{0} motor.\nL104: During ATP synthesis, the electrochemical ion gradient generated during respiration, photosynthesis or membrane-bound decarboxylation drives mechanical rotation in the F_{0} part that is transmitted via a central shaft into the nucleotide binding sites in F_{1}, where ATP is formed from ADP and inorganic phosphate. Conversely, excess of ATP causes the F_{1} motor to rotate in the opposite direction.\nL105: This drives ion transport through the F_{0} part to generate an electrochemical potential across the membrane. While the ATP hydrolysis mode is strictly controlled (and thus inhibited) in eukaryotes, it plays an important role during bacterial growth under anaerobic conditions. Consequently, both working directions are physiologically relevant.\nL106: Implicit in Mitchell's chemiosmotic model is the assumption that a membrane potential (Δψ) and a transmembrane proton concentration gradient (ΔpH) or a transmembrane Na^{+} concentration gradient (ΔpNa) are equivalent driving forces for ATP synthesis (cite14†Mitchell, 1979 ).\nL107: This view is reinforced by experiments showing that the chloroplast ATP synthase is driven almost completely by ΔpH (cite15†Fischer and Gräber, 1999 ), while the mitochondrial and bacterial ATP synthases operate on a combination of membrane potential and ΔpH (cite16†Sone et al., 1977 ).\nL108: ATP synthesis experiments with the Na^{+} ATP synthase of Propionigenium modestum and the H^{+} ATP synthase of Escherichia coli, however, established an essential role for the membrane potential (cite15†Fischer and Gräber, 1999 ; cite17†Kaim and Dimroth, 1999 ). This sheds doubt on the perfect equivalence of the two driving forces. Nonetheless, present models for torque generation treat the effects of the two components ambiguously.\nL109: It has to be presumed that different environments on either side of the membrane require specific features for operation of the F_{0} motor in either direction. Recently, conditions for measuring proton transport through F_{0} in hydrolysis and synthesis reaction were established by the unidirectional incorporation of enzyme into liposomes (cite18†Wiedenmann et al., 2008 ).\nL110: It was found that Δψ and ΔpH are equivalent driving forces for the H^{+}-transport through the F_{0} part of E. coli and spinach chloroplasts. Here, with the F_{0} part of the Na^{+}-translocating enzyme of P. modestum, however, we found that ΔpNa is only a driving force for the Na^{+}-transport in synthesis direction, while Δψ can energize Na^{+}-transport in both directions.\nL111: It has been shown that the concentration requirement for the coupling ion during the ATP hydrolysis reaction is comparable to the affinity of the binding site of the non-energized enzyme in solution (cite19†von Ballmoos and Dimroth, 2007 ). In the present study, we exploited advantages of the Na^{+}- and H^{+}-translocating ATP synthases from P. modestum and E. coli respectively, and we also determined the critical parameters to drive ATP synthesis and ATP hydrolysis in liposomes.\nL112: Most surprisingly, we found a requirement for > 60 times higher coupling ion concentrations at the ion source side during ATP synthesis than during ATP hydrolysis in both classes of enzymes, defining a so far underestimated parameter for efficient function of the ATP synthase. These and other critical parameters that were determined for ATP synthesis and ion translocation were incorporated into a feasible model for torque generation in the F_{0} motor.\nL113: Finally, implications of the results for the ATP synthesis in vivo are discussed.\nL114: ## Results\nL115: ### ATP synthesis with the reconstituted ATP synthase of P. modestum\nL116: To investigate the influence of each component of the ion motive force, the membrane potential and the ion concentration gradient, on ATP synthesis without interference by other components, the Na^{+}-translocating ATP synthase of P. modestum was purified and reconstituted unidirectionally into proteoliposomes.\nL117: In a first series of experiments, the proteoliposomes were energized by a K^{+}/valinomycin diffusion potential of ∼160 mV, and ΔpNa was varied by applying constant internal and variable external Na^{+} concentrations. The results in cite20†Fig. 1A demonstrate that no ATP is synthesized if a Δψ of 160 mV is applied exclusively, but additionally a ΔpNa of at least 30 mV is required (i.e. a total driving force of 190 mV).\nL118: The rate of ATP synthesis increased steadily when the ΔpNa was further increased above this threshold value. When ΔpNa was kept constant around 100 mV and the Δψ was varied from 0 to 180 mV, efficient ATP synthesis required a Δψ > 90 mV, i.e. a total driving force of 190 mV as in the first series of experiments. With increasing Δψ values, the ATP synthesis rates increased rapidly coinciding with the rates from the first series of experiments.\nL119: Hence, at physiological driving forces (> 180 mV) ΔpNa and Δψ are indeed equivalent as proposed by the chemiosmotic theory. In contrast, below a total driving force of 160 mV ATP is only synthesized in the presence of ΔpNa. These data extend earlier findings (cite15†Fischer and Gräber, 1999 ; cite17†Kaim and Dimroth, 1999 ), establishing ΔpNa as indispensible driving force.\nL120: Figure 1\nL121: \nL122: cite11†Open in figure viewer cite21†PowerPoint L123: Requirements for ATP synthesis of the P. modestum enzyme.\nL124: A. Impact of Δψ and ΔpNa. In the first series of experiments (closed circles), a constant Δψ = 160 mV was applied to reconstituted proteoliposomes containing the P. modestum F_{1}F_{0} ATP synthase by a K^{+}/valinomycin diffusion potential (K^{+}_{in} = 0.25 mM and K^{+}_{out} = 100 mM). The ΔpNa was varied by maintaining the internal Na^{+} concentration at 100 mM and changing the NaCl concentration in the assay buffer.\nL125: In the next series (open circles), the ΔpNa was kept at 120 mV (Na^{+}_{in} = 100 mM and Na^{+}_{out} = 1 mM), and varying Δψ values were applied by adding 1 μM valinomycin to proteoliposomes with 0.5, 10 or 25 mM K^{+} in the inside and appropriate KCl concentrations in the assay buffer. Inset: the data are plotted with the y-axis in a logarithmic scale.\nL126: B. Impact of Na^{+} concentration. F_{1}F_{0} proteoliposomes for ATP synthesis measurements (open circles) contained Na^{+} concentrations varying from 0.8 mM to 150 mM and 0.5 mM K^{+}. To generate a constant Δψ = 140 mV and ΔpNa = 108 mV, proteoliposomes were diluted 1:60 into buffer containing 50 μM Na^{+}, 100 mM K^{+} and 1 μM valinomycin. Data for the hydrolysis reaction in proteoliposomes (closed circles) were obtained as described in Experimental procedures.\nL127: C. Na^{+}-affinity of the F_{0} part. Liposomes containing unidirectionally reconstituted F_{0} parts from P. modestum were prepared as described (cite18†Wiedenmann et al., 2008 ) and incubated overnight with Na^{+} concentrations varying from 50 μM to 200 mM in the presence of either 0.5 or 50 mM K_{2}SO_{4}.\nL128: For H^{+}-transport measurements in hydrolysis direction (closed circles), 10 μl of liposomes containing 50 mM K_{2}SO_{4} were mixed with 2.5 ml buffer (2 mM MOPS-Tris, pH 6.5, 0.5 mM K_{2}SO_{4}) of the same Na^{+} concentration, and the transport was initiated by the addition of 16 nM valinomycin (Δψ = 120 mV, negative inside).\nL129: For H^{+}-transport measurements in synthesis direction (open circles), 10 μl of liposomes containing 0.5 mM K_{2}SO_{4} were mixed with 2.5 ml buffer (2 mM MOPS-Tris, pH 6.5, 50 mM K_{2}SO_{4}) of the same Na^{+} concentration, and the transport was initiated by the addition of 16 nM valinomycin (Δψ = 120 mV, positive inside).\nL130: D. Na^{+}-transport through isolated F_{0} in ATP synthesis direction. Unidirectionally oriented F_{0} proteoliposomes containing 0.5 mM or 200 mM KCl were incubated with 100 mM ^{22}NaCl as described under Experimental procedures. To create a ΔpNa alone (filled squares), liposomes containing 200 mM KCl were diluted 1:50 into buffer containing 60 μM NaCl, 200 mM KCl and 1 μM valinomycin.\nL131: For measurements with a Δψ only (filled diamonds), liposomes containing 0.5 mM KCl were diluted 1:50 into buffer containing 200 mM KCl, 100 mM NaCl and 1 μM valinomycin. For measurements with a combination of Δψ and ΔpNa (filled circles), liposomes containing 0.5 mM KCl were diluted 1:50 into buffer containing 200 mM KCl, 60 μM NaCl (background) and 1 μM valinomycin.\nL132: The filled triangles show a control experiment (to that depicted by filled circles), in which the liposomes were treated with 50 μM DCCD for 60 min at 4°C prior to the incubation with ^{22}NaCl. See also Figs S1–S3 for the experimental setup.\nL133: To investigate the effect of the Na^{+} concentration on the rate of ATP synthesis, ΔpNa and Δψ were maintained at 90 and 140 mV respectively, and the Na^{+} concentrations on both sides of the liposome membrane were changed in parallel. The results, depicted in cite20†Fig. 1B , show that for efficient ATP synthesis rather high Na^{+} concentrations (app. K_{D}∼35 mM) are required at the source P-side.\nL134: On the other hand, the Na^{+} concentration requirement for ATP hydrolysis in liposomes (fully uncoupled from ion transport by monensin and valinomycin) was much lower (K_{D}∼0.75 mM), although the ion pathway is presumed to be identical (cite22†Laubinger and Dimroth, 1989 ).\nL135: We addressed the question whether the Na^{+} concentration requirement for ATP synthesis is influenced by the membrane potential but found no significant change in the Na^{+} stimulation pattern between Δψ of 100 and 160 mV (cite23†Table 1 ).\nL136: Table 1. Apparent K_{D} values for Na^{+} during ATP synthesis at different electrical potentials.\nL137: \nL138: Δψ  | 100 mV  | 120 mV  | 140 mV  | 160 mV\nL139: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL140: K_{D} (mM)  | 39 ± 5  | 34 ± 6  | 35 ± 3  | 39 ± 5\nL141: \nL142:   * Experimental procedures are similar as described in cite20†Fig. 1B . The ΔpNa^{+} was kept constant at 108 mV during all measurements. The data were fitted using a model for a single ligand binding.\nL143: Under optimal conditions (100 mM Na^{+} at the P-side, 1 mM Na^{+} at the N-side, ΔpNa = 120 mV and Δψ =  160 mV, data not shown), the maximal synthesis rate was ∼15 ATP/s per enzyme, which is 10-fold higher than in previous reports, where lower Na^{+} concentrations (2–5 mM) were used (cite17†Kaim and Dimroth, 1999 ).\nL144: ### Na^{+} binding affinities of P. modestum F_{0} ATP synthase\nL145: The asymmetric Na^{+} concentration requirements for ATP synthesis and hydrolysis described above suggest different Na^{+} binding affinities of the periplasmic and the cytoplasmic ion entry site in the a/c interface. To test this hypothesis, Na^{+} binding to both sides of the reconstituted F_{0} part was assessed.\nL146: For this purpose we took advantage of the property of the P. modestum F_{0} ATP synthase to translocate protons at pH values around 6.5, which is inhibited in the presence of Na^{+} (cite22†Laubinger and Dimroth, 1989 ). Unidirectionally oriented F_{0} liposomes from P. modestum were prepared and used to determine the initial rate of proton transport at varying Na^{+} concentrations in each specific direction.\nL147: The results (cite20†Fig. 1C ) imply that the inhibition of proton transport by Na^{+} in either direction follows saturation curves showing half maximal inhibition at ∼1 mM and ∼15 mM Na^{+} in hydrolysis and synthesis direction respectively. In the absence of Na^{+}, proton transport rates in either direction were comparable.\nL148: ### Driving forces for Na^{+} through F_{0} in each specific direction\nL149: Na^{+}-transport experiments into each specific direction were performed with proteoliposomes, containing unidirectionally reconstituted F_{0} parts of the P. modestum ATP synthase. In ATP hydrolysis direction (from N- to P-side), Na^{+}-transport could only be energized by Δψ and not by ΔpNa (data not shown), confirming previous results (cite24†Kluge and Dimroth, 1992 ).\nL150: When Na^{+}-transport in ATP synthesis direction was determined, either ΔpNa or Δψ was efficient driving forces with an additive effect if applied together (cite20†Fig. 1D ). These data reinforce the functional asymmetry of the F_{0} part of the P. modestum ATP synthase and furthermore show a perfect coincidence in the driving forces supporting ATP synthesis by the F_{1}F_{0} holoenzyme or Na^{+}-transport through the F_{0} part in the proper direction (from P- to N-side).\nL151: ### Both affinities are governed by the c-ring ion binding site residues\nL152: The different affinities for Na^{+} during ATP synthesis or hydrolysis by the P. modestum enzyme could reflect different affinities of the c-ring binding sites at the periplasmic and cytoplasmic entrance route of the ion. Alternatively, an additional low-affinity site in the periplasmic access route might attenuate the apparent Na^{+} binding affinity during ATP synthesis.\nL153: We aimed to distinguish between these possibilities by investigation of a mutant ATP synthase from Ilyobacter tartaricus, which possesses a point mutation (Y70F) in the Na^{+} binding site of the c-ring. The I. tartaricus ATP synthase is closely related to that of P. modestum but offers the possibility of heterologous expression in E. coli.\nL154: Importantly, the heterologously expressed I. tartaricus wild type enzyme has similar Na^{+}-affinities as the P. modestum enzyme for ATP synthesis and ATP hydrolysis (app. K_{D}∼35 mM and 0.75 mM respectively, data not shown and cite19†von Ballmoos and Dimroth, 2007 ) which justifies a direct comparison between enzymes from different sources. The I. tartaricus Y70F mutant exhibits a ∼50-fold lower affinity for Na^{+} stimulation during ATP hydrolysis (app.\nL155: K_{D}∼35 mM) compared with wild-type enzyme because of a distorted H-bonding network of the binding site residues (cite19†von Ballmoos and Dimroth, 2007 ). When we repeated these measurements with the Y70F enzyme incorporated in lipid vesicles, we observed an increased affinity for Na^{+} (K_{D}∼11 mM) that is still ∼15-fold lower compared with the wild type.\nL156: We reasoned that this affinity would be further attenuated in ATP synthesis experiments, if the affinity of the c-ring binding site is affected by the different environments at the two entrance routes of the ion from the N- and P-side respectively and found indeed that about 17 times higher Na^{+} concentrations were required for the synthesis than the hydrolysis of ATP by this particular mutant (app. K_{D} > 190 mM, cite25†Fig. 2 ).\nL157: In the alternative scenario of a second low-affinity Na^{+} site in the P-side access route, the affinity for Na^{+} in stimulating ATP hydrolysis should, however, not be considerably reduced during ATP synthesis by the Y70F mutant affecting the c-ring binding site.\nL158: Figure 2\nL159: \nL160: cite11†Open in figure viewer cite26†PowerPoint L161: \nL162: Impact of Na^{+} concentration on ATP synthesis and ATP hydrolysis in the cY70F mutant. The experiment was performed as described in cite20†Fig. 1C , except that the proteoliposomes contained heterologously expressed enzyme of I. tartaricus, harbouring the mutation cY70F (open circles). Data for the hydrolysis reaction by the same mutant in proteoliposomes (closed circles) were obtained as described in Experimental procedures.\nL163: ### ATP synthesis by the E. coli ATP synthase\nL164: Stimulated by these results, we extended our investigations to the H^{+}-translocating E. coli ATP synthase. The purified enzyme was reconstituted into phospholipid vesicles as described in Experimental procedures. In order to elicit ATP synthesis, liposomes were energized by a constant Δψ of 160 mV through a K^{+}/valinomycin diffusion potential and varying ΔpH values generated through acid-base treatment.\nL165: The liposomes were pre-incubated with buffer of low pH, allowing equilibration of the inner pH to the low pH of the buffer. The acidified liposomes were then rapidly diluted into high-pH buffer containing valinomycin, inducing ΔpH and Δψ simultaneously. ATP synthesis was monitored using the luminescence reaction.\nL166: cite27†Figure 3A and B show the data of ATP synthesis measurements with liposomes that were pre-incubated at pH 5.75 (P-side) and then diluted into buffers with pH values varying from pH 6.25 to 8.75 (N-side), resulting in ΔpH values from 0.5 to 3. The applied Δψ was kept constant at 160 mV. A ΔpH of 0.5 appeared to be insufficient to support detectable ATP synthesis rates.\nL167: Under this condition, the N-side pH is 6.25 and it can not be completely ruled out that this pH is unfavourable for ATP synthesis in the F_{1} part. No ATP synthesis was observed in the absence of Δψ but at ΔpH = 3 (data not shown) which is in accord with earlier findings of Gräber and colleagues that Δψ in addition to ΔpH have to be present for efficient ATP synthesis (cite15†Fischer and Gräber, 1999 ).\nL168: Figure 3\nL169: \nL170: cite11†Open in figure viewer cite28†PowerPoint L171: Requirements for ATP synthesis of the E. coli enzyme.\nL172: A. Time-course of acid-base induced ATP synthesis. A total of 20 μl proteoliposomes were mixed with 20 μl acid buffer (100 mM MES-NaOH, pH 5.75) and incubated for 5 min at room temperature. Meanwhile, 450 μl base buffers (100 mM MOPS-NaOH, pH 6.25–7.25 or 100 mM Tricine-NaOH, pH 7.5–9, 150 mM KCl, 2 mM K_{2}HPO_{4}, 2.5 mM MgCl_{2}) were mixed with 50 μl luciferase reagent, 0.75 mM ADP, 330 nM valinomycin, and a baseline was recorded.\nL173: ATP synthesis was initiated by rapid addition of the proteoliposomes into base buffer to generate a K^{+}/valinomycin diffusion potential of ∼160 mV and the ΔpH values indicated.\nL174: B. Impact of ΔpH on ATP synthesis rate. Initial rates from the traces from cite27†Fig. 3A were calculated and plotted against the ΔpH values.\nL175: C. Impact of pH at the P-side on ATP synthesis. ATP synthesis measurements were performed as described in cite27†Fig. 3A . The ΔpH was kept constant using acid (100 mM MES-HCl, pH 5.75–7) and base buffers (100 mM Tricine-NaOH, pH 7.25–9, plus additives described under 3A) at different pH values. Shown are results for ΔpH = 1.5 (open circles) and ΔpH = 2 (closed circles). The two series were normalized to the values obtained at pH 5.75.\nL176: D. The pH dependency of H^{+}-transport through the isolated F_{0} part, reconstituted into proteoliposomes. Initial rates of H^{+}-transport through unidirectionally oriented F_{0} parts were determined as described (cite18†Wiedenmann et al., 2008 ). Shown are the pH dependencies of transport from the N- to P-side (hydrolysis direction, closed circles) and from the P- to N-side (synthesis direction, open circles). See also Figs S4 and S5 for the experimental setup.\nL177: Analogously to Na^{+} in the enzyme of P. modestum, we asked whether ATP synthesis by the E. coli ATP synthase was dependent not only on the two driving forces but also on the absolute P-side proton concentration. To address this question, ATP synthesis experiments were performed at a constant Δψ of 160 mV and a constant ΔpH of 1.5 (∼90 mV) but varying pH values on both sides.\nL178: The results in cite27†Fig. 3C show that ATP synthesis rates decreased continuously with increasing the P-side pH from 5.75 to 7.0 (and increasing the N-side pH from 7.25 to 8.5 to maintain a ΔpH of 1.5), where synthesis was completely abolished. This finding is remarkable, as ATP hydrolysis is not significantly affected by the pH between 6.5 and 9 (data not shown and cite19†von Ballmoos and Dimroth, 2007 ).\nL179: Hence, the E. coli ATP synthase exhibits similar asymmetric coupling ion affinities during ATP synthesis and hydrolysis as the P. modestum enzyme.\nL180: ### Proton transport through the isolated F_{0} part of the E. coli ATP synthase\nL181: To investigate whether the observed asymmetry is apparent in the isolated F_{0} part of the E. coli ATP synthase, initial H^{+}-transport rates were determined in each specific direction (cite27†Fig. 3D ). In hydrolysis direction, the F_{0} liposomes catalysed Δψ-driven proton transport between pH 6 and 9 at a constant rate, resembling the pH profile during ATP hydrolysis (cite19†von Ballmoos and Dimroth, 2007 ).\nL182: H^{+}-transport in ATP synthesis direction, however, decreased continuously with increasing pH values from 6 to 9 to less than 20% of the initial rate. Thus, the pH profiles for ATP synthesis and H^{+} conduction in the appropriate direction resemble each other except for a shift of the apparent pK_{a} from 6.2 to 7.7. This shift may be related to the demand for increased torque during ATP synthesis, where counteracting conformational restraints in F_{1} have to be overcome.\nL183: ### ATP synthesis driven by primary proton pumps\nL184: The requirement of a P-side pH < 6.5 for ATP synthesis in E. coli is in good accord with numerous experiments, where ATP synthesis was driven by an acid-base transition, in which the P-side pH was intuitively adjusted to values below 6.5 (usually below pH 5). However, when ATP synthesis by inverted membrane vesicles was energized by the respiratory chain, the P-side pH could be varied between 6.5 and 8.\nL185: In these vesicles, the ATP synthase has the same orientation as in our reconstituted proteoliposomes and the requirements for ATP synthesis should be comparable. To elucidate details of the ATP synthesizing machinery, we followed ATP synthesis, nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide (NADH) oxidation, membrane potential and intravesicular pH in strongly buffered (100 mM) inverted E. coli membrane vesicles in parallel (cite29†Fig. 4A ).\nL186: Respiratory proton pumping initiated by NADH addition (or succinate, yielding similar results) led to the formation of a membrane potential which was fully established in an initial phase of < 10 s and declined when NADH oxidation slowed down (cite29†Fig. 4B ). Respiration also initiated ATP synthesis which reached maximal rates after the initial phase in which the membrane potential was generated and stopped after NADH oxidation had ceased.\nL187: A decrease of the internal pH (from pH 7.25 to pH 7.15) was also observed, of which 80% were established within the first 8 s and which declined at the end of NADH consumption. The observed acidification (corresponding to 0.1 pH units), however, does not suffice to establish the proton concentration required for ATP synthesis as determined in our in vitro experiments (< pH 6.5, cite27†Fig. 3C ).\nL188: The discrepancy between the pH required for ATP synthesis and that monitored by our measuring system can be accounted for if the pH drop by the pumped protons is asymmetrically distributed with the maximum effect at the membrane surface and only minor contributions from the bulk. To test this hypothesis, further experiments are required which allow determination of local proton concentrations on the membrane surface of respiring vesicles.\nL189: Figure 4\nL190: \nL191: cite11†Open in figure viewer cite30†PowerPoint L192: Time resolved monitoring of ATP synthesis in inverted vesicles of E. coli.\nL193: A. Experimental setup. Shown are inverted membrane vesicles, containing the NADH quinone oxidoreductase (complex I) and the ATP synthase in the appropriate orientation. Green stars indicate the homogenous distribution of the fluorescent pH indicator pyranine which served to monitor the internal pH of the vesicles.\nL194: B. Time traces of critical parameters during ATP synthesis. Shown are traces of NADH oxidation (1), ATP synthesis (2), membrane potential (3) and intravesicular pH (4). The reaction was initiated by addition of 20 μM NADH. Indicated are an initial phase (0–8 s, yellow) and a late phase (8–45 s, brown). See text for details.\nL195: ## Discussion\nL196: ### Requirements for ATP synthesis\nL197: During bacterial ATP synthesis, coupling ions are taken up from the periplasm through the P-side pathway in subunit a and delivered to a c-ring binding site, which is in contact with this access pathway. From there, the freshly occupied binding site moves out of the a/c interface and releases its ion through the N-side pathway to the cytoplasm after an almost complete revolution.\nL198: When the enzyme works in the ATP hydrolysis mode, ions are taken up from the N-side and delivered to the P-side, while the c-ring rotates in the opposite direction as during ATP synthesis. For Na^{+}-translocating enzymes, it has been found that ATP hydrolysis is dependent on the presence of Na^{+}. The apparent affinity is ∼0.5–1 mM Na^{+}, which is in good agreement with the affinity of the binding site for Na^{+} in the isolated c-ring (cite31†Meier et al., 2003 ).\n"
